#host("The Muslim Lantern", ("For instance, they will talk about inheritance. You know, the man gets two times the woman in the inheritance. In the reality, this is utterly false. You know, there's four cases where inheritance is distributed in Islam. One case is where a man would take two times the woman and this is in cases, for example, where you have a daughter and a son. Why? Because there's a huge financial burden on the son. Like we were saying, you have to provide clothing, housing, you have to provide a marital gift, you have to take care of the woman financially for the rest of her life.", "So, you have more of a financial burden as a man. So, it makes sense that you take more in that case. However, there are cases where they take the same. For example, there are cases where the mother and the father both, men and women, they will take sixth of the inheritance. So, they take equally. There is now another type of cases where women will inherit more than men. So, for example, if you have only a daughter, a daughter takes half of the inheritance. Two daughters, they take two-thirds of the inheritance. Father is there, he doesn't take anywhere near that.", "So, it's not about men or women. There's even cases where women will inherit, and if it was a man in that same case, he wouldn't inherit. So, how is the religion made for the desire of the man? Prohibiting men in inheritance in some cases and giving it to the woman, and having more cases where women are getting more than the men. If a woman dies and leaves behind a husband, a father, and a mother, a daughter, and a daughter of a son. If it's a daughter of a son, she will take sixth of the inheritance.", "But if it's a son of a son, he will take nothing. So, it's the same exact scenario, but it's a man, and the man would not take anything, and the daughter would take something. So, this idea that in inheritance, Islam is just giving more to the man, is is utterly false, right?",))
