#host("The Muslim Lantern", ("No, Hijab in the Arabic language means a barrier between two objects, let's say something that does حَجْب, it becomes a barrier between you and something else Now, a lot of people try to say: Men's Hijab. And they use these terminologies, because they are afraid Of saying women should wear the Hijab. So they want to make it as nice as possible by saying: Ok, a man has Hijab! It's a very simple thing. The term Hijab has been used historically to refer to women And it's not just referring to the headscarf. Hijab is not a head covering.", "That's خِمار (Khimar: long headscarf), you know? Khimar is غطاء الرأس, that which you cover your head with. Hijab is, we're saying you have a barrier between you and the opposite gender, when it comes to the covering; because that can be now, not just clothing and covering, but also behaviour But when it comes to the clothing and covering, the woman has to cover. There are obviously two major opinions between the scholars here: But the least she has to cover is everything except her face and hands So the neck has to be covered, the hair is covered, the hands until here الكفّين, These two (Palms) And the face, this can be left to the opinion of some scholars.", "Some scholars say even those should be covered But this is a valid disagreement, so if a woman doesn't cover them, there are no issues for her from an Islamic point of view Now what she covers with cannot be something which is see-through, cannot be something which is too tight, that shows her body figure; like women who wear jeans and these types of things, right? It defeats the purpose, and it has to be loose basically on covering her body. And now when it comes to also.. There is no point! You cannot show your face and put makeup on.", "That also defeats the purpose, The whole idea is to have a barrier between you and the opposite gender –The people who can get married to you– So for you to cover the body and then put tons of makeup –put your face and makeup on your face– defeats the purpose, right? It's not what Islam is telling you to do. But also the behaviour as well is an important thing Like lowering the gaze; And this is for both men and women (Annur24:30-31) Meaning you have to lower your gaze, your eyes when you're looking, you don't gaze at the opposite gender, etc And now also the speech, the way you speak.", "When you speak with the opposite gender, the things that you say; cannot be things which entices the opposite gender or rub them the wrong way, as some people say. So the Hijab is that, is this whole idea Now there's something called the العورة ('Awrah) which is that which you cannot show from your body that which you cannot show from your body What we just said about women is that they have to cover everything, So everything else they cannot show, it would be considered 'Awrah from that aspect. For the man, he has to cover from the belly button until the knee here.", "This is a must covering you have to cover it We're talking about the must now. You have to cover this as a man. That would be considered a part where you have to cover as a man And obviously yes, there is a different teaching here between men and women, because men and women are different And what men are attracted to, is different than what women are attracted to. So, it doesn't make sense for them to do the exact same thing. So these are the Islamic teachings, but we would not call that necessarily \"Hijab\" like some people try to do I don't agree with that type of terminology for men to say it's a Hijab, essentially",))
