#host("The Muslim Lantern", ("So what should we do as Muslims? If let's say I have.. A child or have a kid, daughter or son that decide to leave Islam or do something like that? Now, obviously the initial first step is to understand where does this come from? What's the cause? I mean, if you did your job as a parent, and taught your children the religion from the beginning, There MUST be something going on that you don't see Now, most cases, actually, it's the fault of the PARENTS Because they didn't do enough effort teaching their children, Don't see who the children are friends with, They take them to different places..", "They focus on other things rather than teaching their children their faith! So most of the time, it's actually the fault of the parents But even if it is, or it isn't the fault of the parents, there's ALWAYS a cause So you need to sit down with your kid You need to sit down in a merciful way with your child, and try to understand why they left the faith Why did they leave the religion? And then you'll try to speak to them, answer their questions If you're not qualified, you'd bring someone to answer the questions You'll try with them in every possible way, And try to fix the problem with a loving attitude towards your child Now, in the case that your child continues in this behaviour, –Now that's a very important thing to understand– Because for Muslims, for us, the connection of religion is stronger than the connection of blood", "The connection of religion is stronger than the connection of blood Allah says in the Quran (alHujurat49:10) (Verily, the believers are brothers) Every Muslim, 2 billion people, EVERY Muslim in the world is my brother or sister, Stronger than the connection of blood that I would have with my own brother or my own sister This is our tradition. This is what we believe as Muslims So if that child, let's say, persists. You tried everything They just don't want anything that has to do with the religion They want to stay on disbelief They are arrogantly remaining in that position They don't care what you've done for them Now, can you Islamically disown that child?", "Yes, you can! Yes, you can I mean, that is our red line as a Muslim. We can do this We can say to that kid, and it's not only for leaving Islam. It could be other Major Sins It could be for other major issues And if they do that, you're allowed to do that And when we say disown, it just depends on what the person means by disowning We're saying, removing communication with them, cutting communication with that person Yes, that is your right! You are allowed to do that in Islam Why? What's the point? Because you've tried everything with them They're not listening, they're remaining in a path of evil So you hope that this disconnection and disowning gives them a wake-up call Let them reconsider their position It's one of the methods of advising someone, And we do believe as Muslims that you're allowed to do that", "And you know, in the Quran –for instance, when I say this connection of blood versus the connection of religion– In the Quran, Allah gives us an example of Noah with his son I mean, Allah told Noah that he's going to save his family (Hud11:40) And then when the flood happened, Noah called upon God (Hud11:45-46) And then Noah speaking to Allahﷻ and he said: (Oh Allah, my son is from my family) وَإِنَّ وَعۡدَكَ ٱلۡحَقُّ (And your promise is the Truth) وَأَنتَ أَحۡكَمُ ٱلۡحَـٰكِمِینَ (And you're the Wisest of Judges) So Noah 'alayhi salam (peace be upon him) called upon God And he said: Oh Allah, that my son is from my family.", "And now he's going to drown And you promised me that you're going to save my family Then Allah responded to him: إِنَّهُۥ لَیۡسَ مِنۡ أَهۡلِكَۖ إِنَّهُۥ عَمَلٌ غَیۡرُ صَـٰلِحࣲۖ Allah said: (He is not from your family. He is unrighteous deed) That's how Allahﷻ described his blood son, if you want to use that blood relationship So when Allah says he's not from your family, meaning he's not from your religion from your faith He is unrighteous deed, meaning, he committed disbelief He associated with God, he worshipped other than God, he disbelieved in the Creator He's not from your family anymore He's not from this connection that you have that we described that is thicker than blood So to us as Muslims, yes!", "the religion is thicker than blood So what Chunkz was saying, like I said, it needed a little bit of refinement, Explaining at first, no, you will take a merciful stance with your child First, try to recognise the reason, because there's a lot of miscommunication There's a lot of ignorance There's a lot of, doubts that are spread around that can confuse someone But once someone does every way and everything possible and tries his best with his son or daughter, And they still remain on arrogance and disbelief Yes! that is something you should do as a Muslim, actually Like, as I said, as a form of admonishing and giving advice to that kid, hoping..", "Hopefully, that the reason you're doing it; is hoping that they will wake up And they come back to the path of the Truth Because to us as Muslims, if they die as disbelievers, then they've lost everything And eternality of Hellfire –me disowning him or not disowning him– If it that helps my child, he's not going to spend an eternality in Hellfire, Yes! I would do that. Absolutely And this is our red line, like I said So may Allahﷻ guide us all, and inshaAllah keep us all firm on the Truth, And if this were to happen -like I said- with you or your family members or your friends, Take the right approach.", "Don't take the wrong approach in the beginning No! Take the right approach, because I know many communities and societies, They will instantly go to this point And that can have more of a negative effect on the child, because they're confused They're doubtful, there's an issue, rather than taking it in a merciful, lenient way That actually will bring them closer, you actually push them further away from the Faith So hopefully that makes sense, inshaAllah (God willing)",))
